    <div class="page-body">

      <p>
        Technical Artist and 3D generalist with a decade in AAA and indie games across Tech Art
        and QA Engineering, at Riot Games, Epic Games, Left Turn Studios, and Bungie. The work covers shader
        authoring, VFX, animation pipelines, asset optimization, tooling, and procedural
        content: broad scope on small teams, deep technical ownership on large ones. Comfortable
        in Unity and Unreal Engine, and quick to get up to speed in an unfamiliar codebase or
        tool when the project needs it.
      </p>

      <p>
        The combination is a little unusual. Artistic training at DigiPen gave a foundation in
        3D craft and a standard for what the work should look like. Underneath that is a
        mindset that has driven every role: wanting to know why things work the way they do,
        solving problems at the root, and making other people's work easier. It's what made
        QA engineering a good fit, and years of it at Bungie and Epic honed skills that carry
        straight into tech art: investigating problems across disciplines, knowing where a
        pipeline is likely to break, and explaining it clearly to the people who can fix it.
        The result is tech art that doesn't stop at looking good: pipelines that hold, tooling
        that other people can actually use, and documentation that means the knowledge
        doesn't walk out the door. The intersection of engineering precision and
        artistic craft is where the work lives.
      </p>

      <p>
        Currently a Senior Technical Artist at Riot Games on Teamfight Tactics, building tools
        and an agentic AI framework for artists and Tech Artists during the game's move to
        Unreal Engine 5. AI tooling is part of the craft, not a shortcut: a way to build things
        faster with more deliberate craft than a solo developer could manage alone. The <a href="/how-i-work/">How I Work</a>
        page gets into the specifics of how that collaboration actually functions and what it
        produces.
      </p>

